Pairing and the projections on the list domain are computable.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`Proposition77.proj1Map`).** The second projection relation sends the master neighbourhood to every master target and sends a paired neighbourhood to targets containing its right component.

*Proof.* The relation contains the master pair, is closed under target intersections, and is monotone under refinement of source and enlargement of target. ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.proj1Map_rel`).** The second projection relates \(W\) to \(Z\) exactly when both are list neighbourhoods and either \(Z\) is master or \(W\) is a pair whose right component is contained in \(Z\).

*Proof.* This is the defining relation. ∎

**Definition (`Proposition77.pairSharpMap`).** The pairing relation sends a product neighbourhood generated by \(A,B\) to every list neighbourhood containing the embedded pair of \(A\) and \(B\).

*Proof.* Pairing master neighbourhoods gives the master case; intersections and monotonicity follow componentwise. ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.pairSharpMap_rel`).** A product neighbourhood \(V\) is related to \(W\) exactly when \(V\) is generated by list neighbourhoods \(A,B\) and the embedded pair of \(A,B\) is contained in \(W\).

*Proof.* This is the defining relation. ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.pairSharpMap_toElementMap`).** On elements, the pairing approximable map is the list-domain pairing operation.

*Proof.* A target neighbourhood is obtained from component neighbourhoods exactly when it contains their embedded pair. ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.pairSharp_rel_Vsharp_iff`).** At presentation indices \(t,k\), the pairing relation holds exactly when the paired list neighbourhood indexed by \(2t+2\) is contained in the neighbourhood indexed by \(k\).

*Proof.* Decode \(t\) into its two product indices and use the even-index formula for list neighbourhoods. ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.pairSharp_isComputable`).** The pairing approximable map on the list domain is computable.

*Proof.* Its indexed relation reduces to decidable inclusion after the primitive-recursive transformation \((t,k)\mapsto(2t+2,k)\). ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.proj1_rel_Vsharp_iff`).** The second projection relates the neighbourhood indexed by \(m\) to that indexed by \(k\) exactly when \(k\) is the master index, or \(m\) is nonzero and even and its decoded right component is contained in \(k\).

*Proof.* Separate the master, zero-embedded, and paired forms of the source; only a paired source has a right component. ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.proj1_isComputable`).** The second projection approximable map is computable.

*Proof.* The criterion uses decidable tests for zero, parity, and nonzeroness, together with decidable inclusion of the decoded right component. ∎

**Theorem (`Proposition77.proj1_toElementMap_pairSharp`).** Applying the second projection to a paired list-domain element returns its second component.

*Proof.* A target neighbourhood belongs to the image exactly when it contains a right-component neighbourhood of the pair. ∎
